            "column": 3
          },
          "text": "Migrating a Web App Using Cordova to Capacitor: A Step-by-Step Guide"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": {
        "line": 4,
        "column": 1
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 4,
            "column": 1
          },
          "text": "This guide will help you migrate your web app from Cordova to Capacitor, making it easy to read and follow. We'll cover all sections and provide a step-by-step approach."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "location": {
        "line": 6,
        "column": 1
      },
      "depth": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 6,
            "column": 4
          },
          "text": "Introduction to Cordova and Capacitor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": {
        "line": 8,
        "column": 1
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 8,
            "column": 1
          },
          "text": "Cordova and Capacitor are both tools that enable web developers to create native applications for various platforms using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. While they share similarities, there are key differences in their approach to native project management, plugin management, and CLI/version management."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "location": {
        "line": 10,
        "column": 1
      },
      "depth": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 10,
            "column": 4
          },
          "text": "Migration Strategy"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": {
        "line": 12,
        "column": 1
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 12,
            "column": 1
          },
          "text": "Migrating from Cordova to Capacitor can be done gradually or as a complete replacement, depending on the complexity of your app. Capacitor is backward-compatible with Cordova, allowing you to switch your existing web apps to it whenever you're ready."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "location": {
        "line": 14,
        "column": 1
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 14,
            "column": 1
          },
          "text": "To assist with migration, consider using the "
        },
        {
          "type": "link",
          "location": {
            "line": 14,
            "column": 46
          },
          "url": "https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items/?itemName=ionic.ionic",
          "title": null,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "location": {
                "line": 14,
                "column": 47
              },
              "text": "Ionic VS Code Extension"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 14,
            "column": 137
          },
          "text": " and auditing your existing Cordova plugins. You can continue to use Cordova plugins if needed, or replace them with Capacitor equivalents."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "location": {
        "line": 16,
        "column": 1
      },
      "depth": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "location": {
            "line": 16,
            "column": 4
          },
          "text": "Step-by-Step Migration Guide"
        }
      ]
